---
// Lesson 1.3a — Your First Script: Variables & Update
// A brand-new script (PlayerLogger.cs), not the shipped PlayerController:
// class syntax, [SerializeField], Update(), Debug.Log.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.3a — Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Today you write C# that runs inside your game. Not the player's code —
    that already works. Your own tiny script, from scratch, so the big
    one stops being magic.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Open <code>Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs</code> and look at the
    skeleton that runs on the Player right now:</p>
    <pre><code>public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    private Animator anim;

    private void Start()
    {
        anim = GetComponentInChildren&lt;Animator&gt;();
        if (anim != null)
        {
            anim.SetBool("IsWalking", true);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>Notice what is here — and what is missing. There is a <code>Start()</code> method
    that grabs the Animator and starts the walk cycle, but there is no <code>Update()</code>
    method yet! Predict before running anything: if you add an <code>Update()</code> method
    with <code>Debug.Log("hello")</code> inside it, roughly how many times does "hello"
    print in ten seconds? Ten? One? Something much bigger? Write your answer down — we check
    it after you build your own script.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            The class name is <code>PlayerController</code> and the file is
            PlayerController.cs. Why does Unity force that match, when a
            regular program would happily let you rename a file?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — create the script.</strong> In the Project
    window, right-click Assets/Scripts → Create → C# Script (or, in
    newer Unity: Create → Scripting → MonoBehaviour Script). Name it
    <code>PlayerLogger</code> — the file must match the class exactly.
    Open it. Unity writes you a starter that gives you two methods:
    <code>Start</code> and <code>Update</code>.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — add a field.</strong> Inside the class, above
    the methods:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float reportDelay = 1.0f;
private float nextReportTime = 0f;</code></pre>
    <p>
        A normal private field is the script's secret. But prefixing it with
        <code>[SerializeField]</code> instructs Unity to expose it as an editable
        knob in the Inspector window without making it accessible to other scripts.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — print on a timer.</strong> In Update:</p>
    <pre><code>private void Update()
{
    if (Time.time &gt;= nextReportTime)
    {
        Debug.Log("Still here at " + Time.time);
        nextReportTime = nextReportTime + reportDelay;
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — attach it.</strong> Drag PlayerLogger.cs onto
    the Player in the Hierarchy (or Add Component → Player Logger).
    Scripts are components — attaching is how a GameObject gets the
    ability. Press Play and read the Console.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the logger on purpose</h3>
        <p>Make each bug, run it, explain the Console, then fix it:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Rename the class <code>playerLogger</code> (lowercase p)
                in the code only. What does Unity say when you press Play?</li>
            <li>Drag the script onto the Turret instead of the Player and
                play. Does it still print — and what does that tell you
                about which object a script belongs to?</li>
            <li>Change the condition to <code>if (Time.time &gt; nextReportTime + reportDelay)</code>.
                What changed about the rhythm, and can you explain why?</li>
            <li>Delete the whole <code>nextReportTime</code> update line.
                What fills the Console, and why is that a problem for
                your first answer in the Predict block?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Make the logger useful</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Make it print the player's position instead of the time —
                Google is allowed: search "unity transform.position". What
                kind of value does <code>transform.position</code> hold?</li>
            <li>Change reportDelay to 0.25 in the Inspector while playing.
                Does the change survive after you press stop, and why?</li>
            <li>Give the logger a second [SerializeField] int called
                maxReports and stop logging after that many. Where did you
                put the check — and would a while loop have worked here
                instead?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Create a new C# script and attach it to a GameObject</li>
            <li>Explain what runs, when: Start once vs Update every frame</li>
            <li>Write a [SerializeField] private field and tune it in the Inspector</li>
            <li>Explain the difference between a class name and a file name</li>
            <li>Use Debug.Log + a timer instead of spamming every frame</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — scripts, variables, input, vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the API tables</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.4a.html">1.4a — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.3a" title="1.3a Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
